{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,10]],"date-time":"2026-10-10T01:49:55Z","timestamp":1791596995477,"version":"4.3.4"},"reference-count":33,"publisher":"Institute of Mathematical Statistics","issue":"5","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Ann. Probab."],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2006,9,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>We study a class of stochastic control problems where a cost of the form $$\\mathbb{E}\\int_{[0,\\infty)}e^{-\\beta s}[\\ell(X_{s})\\,ds+h(Y^{\\circ}_{s})\\,d|Y|_{s}]$$ is to be minimized over control processes Y whose increments take values in a cone $\\mathbb{Y}$ of \u211dp, keeping the state process X=x+B+GY in a cone $\\mathbb{X}$ of \u211dk, k\u2264p. Here, $x\\in\\mathbb{X}$, B is a Brownian motion with drift b and covariance \u03a3, G is a fixed matrix, and Y\u25cb is the Radon\u2013Nikodym derivative dY\/d|Y|. Let \u2112=\u2212(1\/2)trace(\u03a3D2)\u2212b\u22c5D where D denotes the gradient. Solutions to the corresponding dynamic programming PDE, $$[(\\mathscr{L}+\\beta)f-\\ell]\\vee\\sup_{y\\in\\mathbb{Y}:|Gy|=1}[-Gy\\cdot Df-h(y)]=0,$$ on $\\mathbb{X}^{o}$ are considered with a polynomial growth condition and are required to be supersolution up to the boundary (corresponding to a \u201cstate constraint\u201d boundary condition on $\\partial\\mathbb{X}$). Under suitable conditions on the problem data, including continuity and nonnegativity of \u2113 and h, and polynomial growth of \u2113, our main result is the unique viscosity-sense solvability of the PDE by the control problem\u2019s value function in appropriate classes of functions. In some cases where uniqueness generally fails to hold in the class of functions that grow at most polynomially (e.g., when h=0), our methods provide uniqueness within the class of functions that, in addition, have compact level sets. The results are new even in the following special cases: (1) The one-dimensional case k=p=1, $\\mathbb{X}=\\mathbb{Y}=\\mathbb{R}_{+}$; (2) The first-order case \u03a3=0; (3) The case where \u2113 and h are linear. The proofs combine probabilistic arguments and viscosity solution methods. Our framework covers a wide range of diffusion control problems that arise from queueing networks in heavy traffic.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1214\/009117906000000359","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2006,11,15]],"date-time":"2006-11-15T16:28:29Z","timestamp":1163608109000},"page":"1864-1909","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":15,"title":["Singular control with state constraints on unbounded domain"],"prefix":"10.1214","volume":"34","author":[{"given":"Rami","family":"Atar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Amarjit","family":"Budhiraja","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"108","reference":[{"key":"30","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","unstructured":"Rockafellar, R. T. (1970). <i>Convex Analysis<\/i>. Princeton Univ. Press.","DOI":"10.1515\/9781400873173"},{"key":"24","unstructured":"Karatzas, I. and Shreve, S. E. (1991). <i>Brownian Motion and Stochastic Calculus<\/i>, 2nd ed. Springer, New York."},{"key":"20","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","unstructured":"Ishii, H. (1989). On uniqueness and existence of viscosity solutions of fully nonlinear second-order elliptic PDEs. <i>Comm. Pure Appl. Math.<\/i> <b>42<\/b> 15\u201345.","DOI":"10.1002\/cpa.3160420103"},{"key":"17","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","unstructured":"Harrison, J. M. (2000). Brownian models of open processing networks: Canonical representation of workload. <i>Ann. Appl. Probab.<\/i> <b>10<\/b> 75\u2013103.","DOI":"10.1214\/aoap\/1019737665"},{"key":"3","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","unstructured":"Bohm, V. (1975). On the continuity of the optimal policy set for linear programs. <i>SIAM J. Appl. Math.<\/i> <b>28<\/b> 303\u2013306.","DOI":"10.1137\/0128026"},{"key":"5","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","unstructured":"Bramson, M. and Williams, R. J. (2003). Two workload properties for Brownian networks. <i>Queueing Systems<\/i> <b>45<\/b> 191\u2013221.","DOI":"10.1023\/A:1027372517452"},{"key":"11","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","unstructured":"Dupuis, P. and Ishii, H. (1991). On Lipschitz continuity of the solution mapping to the Skorokhod problem, with applications. <i>Stochastics Stochastics Rep.<\/i> <b>35<\/b> 31\u201362.","DOI":"10.1080\/17442509108833688"},{"key":"18","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","unstructured":"Harrison, J. M. and Taksar, M. I. (1983). Instantaneous control of Brownian motion. <i>Math. Oper. Res.<\/i> <b>8<\/b> 439\u2013453.","DOI":"10.1287\/moor.8.3.439"},{"key":"19","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","unstructured":"Harrison, J. M. and Van Mieghem, J. A. (1997). Dynamic control of Brownian networks: State space collapse and equivalent workload formulation. <i>Ann. Appl. Probab.<\/i> <b>7<\/b> 747\u2013771.","DOI":"10.1214\/aoap\/1034801252"},{"key":"1","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","unstructured":"Bardi, M. and Capuzzo-Dolcetta, I. (1997). <i>Optimal Control and Viscosity Solutions of Hamilton\u2013Jacobi\u2013Bellman equations<\/i>. Birkh\u00e4user, Boston.","DOI":"10.1007\/978-0-8176-4755-1"},{"key":"2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","unstructured":"Bene\u0161,V. E., Shepp, L. A. and Witsenhausen, H. S. (1980\/81). Some solvable stochastic control problems. <i>Stochastics<\/i> <b>4<\/b> 39\u201383.","DOI":"10.1080\/17442508008833156"},{"key":"4","unstructured":"Borkar, V. (1989). <i>Optimal Control of Diffusion Processes<\/i>. Longman Scientific and Technical, Harlow."},{"key":"6","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","unstructured":"Capuzzo-Dolcetta, I. and Lions, P. L. (1990). Hamilton\u2013Jacobi equations with state constraints. <i>Trans. AMS<\/i> <b>318<\/b> 643\u2013683.","DOI":"10.1090\/S0002-9947-1990-0951880-0"},{"key":"7","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","unstructured":"